# COBYLA

COBYLA is a local derivative-free optimizer for constrained scalar functions.
It estimates the objective and constraints by linear interpolation, then
balances objective reduction against the largest constraint violation. You
supply function values, and the starting point may be infeasible.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/cobyla/struct.Cobyla.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                 |
  | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A scalar function; smoothness helps the linear models         |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and values for declared nonlinear blocks       |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                               |
  | Constraints | Bounds, linear blocks, nonlinear equalities, and nonlinear inequalities |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`                  |

## When to use it

Consider COBYLA when the objective or constraints lack reliable derivatives and
you need a method that can recover from an infeasible start. Powell's [1994
chapter](#ref-powell-1994-cobyla) introduces the linear interpolation method.
Smoothness helps those models predict local behavior, although Basin does not
require differentiability.

If all constraints are linear and the objective is smooth, compare
[LINCOA](../lincoa/), which builds a quadratic objective model. For box bounds
alone, compare [BOBYQA](../bobyqa/). For a nonsmooth problem with nonlinear
inequalities, compare the progressive-barrier mode of [MADS](../mads/).

COBYLA permits infeasible trial points, including points outside box bounds.
Use it when your callbacks can evaluate those points. Its local search also
means that different starts can find different constrained solutions.

## How it works

At each stage, COBYLA maintains a simplex of `n + 1` points for `n` parameters.
The objective and each constraint have a linear interpolating model on those
vertices. A trust-region subproblem proposes a step using the models; geometry
steps repair the simplex when it becomes poorly shaped.

The method uses a merit function of the form `objective + penalty * violation`,
where the violation is the largest positive constraint residual. The penalty
changes as the method balances reducing the objective and improving
feasibility. The resolution `rho` shrinks toward the configured final radius.

Basin folds bounds and linear and nonlinear blocks into residuals with the
convention `c(x) <= 0`. An equality contributes residuals of opposite signs.
The rows are neither normalized nor relaxed. The current implementation
interpolates every constraint model, including linear constraints.

## Example

Minimizing `x[0] * x[1]` inside the unit disk has minimum value `-0.5`. The
example declares one nonlinear inequality, starts outside the disk, and checks
both the selected objective and its reported violation.

<!-- compile: solver-cobyla -->
```rust
use basin::{
    Cobyla, Constrained, ConstraintSet, ConstraintSolveError, CostFunction,
    Executor, NonlinearInequalityValues, Termination, ViolationMeasure,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Disk;

impl CostFunction for Disk {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x[0] * x[1])
    }
}

impl NonlinearInequalityValues for Disk {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Values = Vec<f64>;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn nonlinear_inequalities(
        &self,
        x: &Self::Param,
    ) -> Result<Self::Values, ConstraintSolveError<Self::Error>> {
        Ok(vec![x[0].powi(2) + x[1].powi(2) - 1.0])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let constraints = ConstraintSet::new().with_nonlinear_inequalities(1);
    let problem = Constrained::new(Disk, constraints);
    let solver = Cobyla::new()
        .with_initial_radius(0.5)
        .with_final_radius(1e-7);

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![1.0, 1.0])
        .max_cost_evals(500)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    let (x, cost, violation) = result.state.best().unwrap();
    assert_eq!(violation.measure(), &ViolationMeasure::LInf);
    assert!(violation.value < 1e-5);
    assert!(x[0].powi(2) + x[1].powi(2) <= 1.0 + 1e-5);
    assert!((cost + 0.5).abs() < 1e-3);
}
```

The constraint callback returns the residual `x[0]^2 + x[1]^2 - 1`, so
nonpositive values mean feasibility. Keep the declared count equal to the
callback's output length. Nonlinear equalities use a separate
`with_nonlinear_equalities` declaration and `NonlinearEqualityValues` callback.

## Practical considerations

### Scale constraint residuals deliberately

The largest positive residual enters the merit function without row
normalization. Multiplying a constraint by a positive constant preserves its
feasible set but changes its influence on the search. Choose residual units
that make the relative importance of violations sensible.

Equalities become paired inequalities. A small final radius does not guarantee
that either residual is small enough for your application. Check each
constraint at the returned point using your own feasibility tolerance.

### Choose radii for the accuracy of your evaluations

The initial radius defaults to `1.0` and the final radius to `1e-6`; require
`initial_radius > final_radius > 0`. Rescale parameters so a single radius
describes useful changes in each coordinate. Tiny radii cannot recover detail
hidden by objective noise, constraint noise, or rounding.

Natural convergence means the final-radius stage finished, not that the
constraints are satisfied. `with_absolute_radius_tolerance` adds an optional
earlier check without changing the algorithm's schedule. Inspect the
termination report together with the violation report.

### Budget objective and constraint calls

`max_cost_evals` limits objective calls. Nonlinear inequality and equality
callbacks count separately as residual evaluations, so use
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::Residual, limit)` when those callbacks have
their own budget. Bound and linear arithmetic require no residual callbacks.

The selected point follows COBYLA's feasibility and merit rules. Its objective
can increase as feasibility improves, so objective-only executor targets and
stall checks are unavailable for this state.

## Implementation

COBYLA publishes the selected point, objective, and `ViolationReport` through
`SelectedState`. The report uses the unscaled `LInf` measure with
`PrimaModeration`: extreme and non-finite callback values undergo PRIMA's
moderation before model construction and reporting. Reported violation is the
maximum positive folded residual, rather than a derivative-based optimality
test.

The solver owns the simplex, penalty, filter, and radius schedule. Retain it
with `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `rho()` or
`geometry_diagnostics()`. Fresh initialization rebuilds those structures;
an `ExactCheckpoint` preserves them for exact continuation.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Nonlinear
values may use a different vector representation from the parameters. Present
linear blocks additionally require the dense matrix capabilities documented
in the API. The simplex calculations use pure-Rust internal buffers, need no
BLAS/LAPACK provider, and work on WASM. Basin ports and cross-validates the
algorithm against [PRIMA](https://github.com/libprima/prima).

